WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.540
Intel has been the biggest name in processor
production for many, many years, but recently,

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.990
they’ve started to lose the race to not
only their main rival, AMD, but to some new

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:11.570
names in the game like Qualcomm, Samsung,
and even Apple.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.610
While there’s a lot that’s happened over
the years that we can’t get into a short

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.270
video like this, let’s quickly go through
some history of Intel’s battle with AMD

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.460
for the PC market.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.110
For many years, AMD CPUs were once seen as
inferior compared to Intel’s CPUs, so they

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.410
started investing their resources trying to
catch up, while building their own tech into

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.410
their CPUs.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.969
And in 2003, AMD released their heavy-hitter
Athlon 64-bit CPUs, which for the first time,

00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:40.039
were superior to Intel’s offerings at the
time, with AMD putting a big focus on gaming.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:44.879
Intel rushed to catch up on 64-bit CPUs, and
it wasn’t until 2006 when they released

00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:49.690
their Core 2 Duo processor, their first dual-core
processors that destroyed the mainstream CPUs

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.069
AMD was offering.

00:00:51.069 --> 00:00:55.929
And shortly after, AMD starting producing
their Athlon X2 dual-core processors, and

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:58.929
then they both ventured into quad-core and
beyond.

00:00:58.929 --> 00:01:02.989
And while all of this was happening, PC gaming
was starting to get really popular, and a

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:07.550
lot of gaming had to decide between a mainstream
Intel processor, or a more budget friendly

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.000
AMD CPU.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.330
Being in my early teens and huge into gaming
back then, I went with AMD’s Phenom 2 955

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.760
Black-edition quad-core processor that I was
able to overclock and run some of my favorite

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.760
games.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.640
And while AMD was focusing on putting out
more cores, Intel decided to mostly stick

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.710
with quad-core processors for many years,
enjoying the bulk of the market share, and

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.270
providing a wide-range of processors for companies
like Apple, from low-end laptop chips to high-end

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.270
Xeon server chips.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.270
Enjoying their success, they weren’t really
investing much into new technology or putting

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.270
out impressive spec bumps with each new processor,
since they didn’t really have to worry about

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.270
the competition too much.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.270
In the meantime, there was some new players
entering the game.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:42.120
In 2011, ARM Holdings announced their first
64-bit mobile chips and in 2013, Apple announced

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.630
the iPhone 5S, the first 64-bit smartphone
packing their own A7 chip.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:50.410
And with smartphones becoming increasingly
more popular, Apple and Android smartphone

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:55.640
makers continued the fight to create the fastest
mobile processors for our phones, but no one

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.409
was expecting mobile chip performance to grow
so quickly.

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:01.380
We’ll dig deeper into that in just a minute.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.170
With AMD being the runner-up to Intel, they
never stopped pushing for newer tech that

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:14.250
could finally disrupt Intel’s market-share
streak, and in 2017, this happened:

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:23.920
AMD released their first line of Ryzen 7 processors,
offering very impressive performance for much

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.730
less cash than Intel’s competing chips.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.920
While this was happening, Intel was already
in trouble, having a very hard time moving

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.560
to their 10nm process.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:38.730
In fact, in 2013, Intel claimed it could produce
10nm chips by 2015.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:45.220
Then they changed it to 2016, and then late
2017, and then late 2019, and here we are

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:51.590
in 2019, and we’re finally seeing some mainstream
10nm chips, which I’ll get into in a moment.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.190
So what was Intel’s response to AMD announcing
Ryzen?

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:59.460
They brought hyper-threading to their i5 and
continued to squeeze out every bit of performance

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.600
they could out of their 14nm process while
beginning to finally lower their prices to

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.560
be able to compete with AMD.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.340
And at the same time, AMD was going full force
with their Ryzen and Threadripper processors,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:17.349
continuing to improve performance while reducing
processor cost compared to Intel’s offerings.

00:03:17.349 --> 00:03:22.140
In 2018, AMD was the best-performing stock
on the S&amp;P 500, and in the past year, it’s

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.030
basically doubled in value, gaining a lot
of attention from mass media and consumers.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:32.400
Around a month ago AMD revealed the specs
of their upcoming 7nm Zen 2 processors at

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.959
Computex 2019, with performance that completely
destroys everything that Intel has to offer,

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:38.560
while using less power and being priced lower.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.489
And just a few days ago, the world's-first
7nm Zen 2 desktop CPUs from AMD are now available

00:03:44.489 --> 00:03:45.489
for purchase.

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:46.489
And they’ve also released very competitive
products in both the mobile APU chip market

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:47.489
and graphics card market as well.

00:03:47.489 --> 00:03:50.120
Shortly after that, an internal Intel memo
was leaked, admitting that AMD’s new Zen

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.220
2 processors will offer great performance
results with prices that are significantly

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.580
below Intel’s, offering good performance-per-dollar.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:02.770
However, Intel also announced their new 10nm
Ice Lake processors at Computex 2019, and

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.900
they’re packing more performance and a lot
of new technology, like integrated Thunderbolt

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:07.989
3.

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:12.629
Their 10nm chips are coming to thin laptops
and tablets first, like Dell’s 2019 XPS

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:14.879
13 2-in-1, which should be coming soon.

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:17.290
Unfortunately, it may already be too late
for Intel.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.519
They’ve been behind for a couple of years
now, giving AMD a chance to swipe up some

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.590
market share, especially since Intel processors
have been experiencing some major security

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.820
issues with the recent Spectre and meltdown
vulnerabilities.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:33.440
Intel’s going to have to come back strong
in the years to come to compete with AMD,

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.430
but I haven’t even talked about their relationship
with Apple, and how they could be losing their

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.880
business very soon.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.260
As many of you may know, Apple’s MacBook
Pros have been experiencing thermal issues

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.530
in recent years, and here’s why.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.520
While Intel was promising that their more
efficient 10nm processors would be coming

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.630
soon, Apple was working on their 2016 redesign
of the MacBook Pro, making it as thin as they

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:56.920
could possibly make it while packing awesome
performance.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:01.580
Unfortunately, Intel’s 10nm processors weren’t
ready for the initial 2016 release of the

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:07.250
MacBook Pro, so Apple had to stick with Intel’s
old 14nm processors, which was fine for the

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:12.070
time being, but Apple and their MacBook Pros
thin and very limited cooling system weren’t

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:14.940
expecting Intel to delay 10nm for so long.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.410
And when Intel started to increase core count
to keep up with AMD’s Ryzen processors,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:24.330
the MacBook Pro started to throttle, really
bad, especially with last years’ Intel’s

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:25.919
6-core i9 processor.

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:29.919
This has caused Apple some very bad press
coverage, pushing users to believe that the

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:32.910
MacBook Pro can’t handle the heat of Intel’s
latest processors.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:37.080
In the meantime, Apple’s mobile ARM chips
have been getting faster and faster every

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:43.060
single year, especially with the release of
their 2018 iPad Pro, packing their A12X processor,

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:48.419
which packed performance that surpassed 92%
of all laptops, tablets and convertible PCs

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:50.290
sold before its release.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.400
So the question a lot of people have is: Why
not just switch to these ARM processors and

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:55.400
ditch Intel?

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.340
Well, that’s exactly what Apple’s been
working on.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:02.820
They started the process by creating their
T2 chip, which takes over a lot of processes

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:07.699
that the Intel processor used to do, and they’ve
been working on making it easier to port iOS

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:12.940
software over to Mac, which will in-turn make
it easier for an ARM processor to run macOS.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:13.940
The biggest challenge will be getting Thunderbolt
3 working with an ARM processor, but just

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:14.940
a few months ago, Apple hired a leading ARM
chip engineer to help them make the switch.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.490
When this happens, it’ll become cheaper
for Apple to produce their Macs, and Intel

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:19.970
will slowly start to lose Apple’s business.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:24.330
And that’s not the worst part, other companies
will follow in Apple’s footsteps and start

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.860
replacing Intel processors with ARM chips.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.190
Their only chance will be to step up their
game and make processors that can compete

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:33.790
with AMD and these up-and-coming powerful
ARM chips.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:37.370
If you enjoyed this video, make sure to tap
the like button and click the circle above

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.370
to subscribe.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:45.550
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next video!

